WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:04.674
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.674 --> 00:00:09.740
I want you to take a moment and picture
the place where you feel most alive.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:15.470
The place where you feel energized,
happiest, maybe in the zone.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.250
Take a moment to look
around your happy place.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.310
And notice what you see,
what you smell, what you hear.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.020
For me, my happy place is a grassy field
in New Hampshire, riding my horse Brutus.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.100
The sun is warm and the air is fresh,
and I take a deep breath and

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.150
feel alert and energized.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:39.870
But also relaxed.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.740
I've asked a lot of people
about their own happy places.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.680
And what I found is that people
prefer all sorts of different places.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.210
Some like it loud, others like it quiet.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.560
Some prefer to be inside, while others
love to be surrounded by nature.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:02.654
But what I have found, is that no
one's happy place looks like this,

00:01:02.654 --> 00:01:04.450
the traditional office.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:08.779
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Today, I want to share with you how

00:01:08.779 --> 00:01:13.500
we can build happier, healthier,
more productive spaces for people.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.180
It's a concept that I've described to
many of you as wellness real estate.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.970
The truth is we spend
a lot of our time indoors.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:24.560
In fact, as Americans,
we spend over 90% of our lives inside.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.770
That means that the built environment

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.730
can have a drastic impact on
our health and well-being.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.500
As future business leaders, I want to
provide you with the information and

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.490
tools you need to build
better organizations.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.350
By understanding what healthier
spaces look and feel like.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:46.900
In an increasingly competitive market
there's never been a better time

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.290
to understand what truly impacts
health and productivity.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.060
For the last five years,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.750
I've been working at companies that
aim to make buildings healthier.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:01.210
The opportunity for impact is massive,
as health is the fastest growing and

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.320
arguably most important market.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.150
And real estate is the world's
largest asset class.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.860
Most recently, I've been working
with the Well Building Standard,

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.610
which is a building certification program
that aims to make buildings healthier.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.030
Through design, operations,
and also policies.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.120
Today I want to share with you my favorite
Well concepts, where I see the greatest

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.090
opportunity for impact, air and light.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:29.880
Let's start with air.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.080
When you think about poor air quality,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.940
you probably imagine
people wearing face masks.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.190
Walking around in smoggy environments
typically in developing cities.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:46.280
Or if you were in California last year
during the fires, you may picture yourself

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.770
walking outside and feeling the soot
in the air hit your nose and eyes.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.003
But even if there isn't smog,
and even if there aren't fires,

00:02:55.003 --> 00:02:57.296
outdoor air quality can be fairly poor.

00:02:57.296 --> 00:03:01.860
And what's concerning is that
indoor air can actually be 2 to 5

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.960
times more contaminated than outdoor air.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.043
And what's concerning about
indoor air is that many of

00:03:09.043 --> 00:03:12.337
the contaminants we find
inside are actually invisible.

00:03:12.337 --> 00:03:17.244
In 2014 Harvard did a study that
brought this idea of healthy indoor

00:03:17.244 --> 00:03:19.462
air quality and work together.

00:03:19.462 --> 00:03:25.018
They did this by studying common office
workers doing common office tasks.

00:03:25.018 --> 00:03:28.650
And looked at how they were responding
in terms of crisis response and

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:29.660
decision making.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.260
They showed that by increasing
the amount of fresh air in a space and

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.000
decreasing the amount of
contaminants in that space.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.107
They could improve
outcomes by nearly 300%.

00:03:41.107 --> 00:03:42.732
How did they do this?

00:03:42.732 --> 00:03:46.934
They started by replacing furniture,
office supplies, and cleaning products,

00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:51.136
with equivalent products that didn't off
get as many contaminants into the indoor

00:03:51.136 --> 00:03:51.930
environment.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.580
They also thought about how much
fresh air was coming through a space.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.140
I'm sure we can all picture ourselves
sitting in a stuffy conference room

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.150
feeling sleepier and sleepier.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:06.200
Or how many of you can picture
yourself in an airplane,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.430
waiting to take off, feeling sleepier and
even a little bit dizzy?

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:17.380
Both of these responses are a result
of too many people in a small space,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.830
breathing out carbon dioxide
without enough fresh oxygen

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:21.709
coming through that space.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:24.790
Luckily, there's an easy solution for

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:28.290
this, called demand
controlled ventilation.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:30.960
With demand control
ventilation sensor sits

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.500
in a potentially densely occupied room.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.480
And as more people come
into that space and

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:38.820
breathe out carbon dioxide,
the building's mechanical system

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.680
automatically pushes more
fresh air into that space.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.960
Now that we're all air experts,
let's talk a little bit about light.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.660
With a show of hands, how many of
you use night shift on your iPhone?.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.050
And for those of you with your hands up,
keep them up there.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:01.866
How many of you know why you use
night shift on your iPhone?.

00:05:01.866 --> 00:05:05.020
All right, about half the room is
an expert already in this space, perfect.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.130
For those of you with your hands up,
you know that our eyes are for

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.070
more than just seeing.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.260
Our eyes, or more specifically,
our optic nerves are responsible for

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.900
perceiving the amount and color
temperature of light in any given space.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.050
Let's take a step back in time
to think about why this is,

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:28.360
back to a time before electricity when we
relied on the sun for most of our light.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:33.920
In the morning as the sun rose,
we saw bright white blue tones of light,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.180
telling our bodies to wake up and
be alert.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.970
And in the afternoon and evening as the
sunset, we saw warmer tones of light or

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.540
golden tones.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.480
Telling our bodies to relax and
start preparing for sleep.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:51.630
This 24 hour sleep wake cycle is
known as our circadian rhythm, and

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.510
it determines many of
our bodily functions.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.980
So what happens when
we disrupt that cycle?

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:01.520
For example, when we see bright white-blue
iPhone light right before we go to sleep?

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:03.820
Well, we're telling our
bodies to wake up and

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.180
be alert,
instead of actually going to sleep.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.540
And we wonder why we have so
many sleep disorders.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.070
And what happens when we sit under
the same office lighting, all day long.

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:18.060
Well, in the US and
in many cities across the world,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.310
typical office lighting is
warm in color temperature.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:25.170
So it's actually telling us to prepare for
sleep all day long.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.490
Luckily, there are easy solutions for
this as well.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.010
Having access to natural daylight can
keep our circadian rhythms in line.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.530
And even when we don't have
access to natural daylight,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.970
we can think about the design
of our overhead lighting.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.780
Or even add a task light to a workstation
that can give us the right amount and

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:45.940
color temperature of
light throughout the day.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:52.810
With this new insight, I want to take
you to another one of my happy places.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:57.600
But first, picture my commute,
a bustling New York City Street,

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.550
stepping off of a crowded subway platform.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:05.868
All of this business disappears as
I step in and out of the elevator,

00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:08.691
into Delos Headquarters, my office.

00:07:08.691 --> 00:07:11.650
What always hits me first is the quiet.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.260
Thanks to carefully designed acoustical
panels on the ceiling, walls, and floor.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:20.648
The space is nearly silent.

00:07:20.648 --> 00:07:25.466
15 active green walls greet me when I walk
in, not only adding greenery to the space,

00:07:25.466 --> 00:07:28.390
but they have special
nodes on their roots.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:30.330
That are pulling contaminants
out of the indoor air.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.640
My arrival has triggered the lights
to turn on bright white-blue

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:35.970
because it's morning.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.680
But I know that over
the course of the day,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.000
they'll fade to a golden color
temperature, helping me prepare for sleep.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.900
I take a deep breath of fresh air and
I feel alert.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.125
I smell a little bit of nature thanks to
the wooden staircase that connects our

00:07:49.125 --> 00:07:49.629
2 floors.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:54.590
I know the workday must begin,
but it doesn't all seem so bad.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.090
As I grabbed organic
banana from the cafe and

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.510
sit down in my ergonomically
adjustable chair.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.394
My mind is clear.

00:08:03.394 --> 00:08:07.432
Now you may be thinking that we can't
all work in an office like Delos, but

00:08:07.432 --> 00:08:10.773
Delos wasn't just designed with
its own employees in mind.

00:08:10.773 --> 00:08:14.653
It was designed as a demonstration space,
to show people what it looks and

00:08:14.653 --> 00:08:19.140
feels like, to be in a space that
was designed with people in mind.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.380
And other major players like CBRE and

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:23.900
even Microsoft they
are implementing wellness design.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.365
You may also be thinking that all
of these seems expensive, but

00:08:29.365 --> 00:08:31.760
luckily it isn't more expensive.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.010
It's simply smarter design.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.020
And even where there
are small increases in cost

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.480
there's a clear return on investment.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:43.100
The American Society of Interior Designers
did a study of their own employees

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:44.950
after moving to a healthier space.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.914
And found increases in
productivity of up to 16%.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:54.210
As future business leaders, we have
the power to make our employees healthier.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:58.680
We can build companies that are not only
more profitable, but also better for

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.080
our people.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.520
And while it's the right thing to do,
it can also make us more competitive.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.170
We can benefit from increases
in productivity, and

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.740
improved attraction, and retention.

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:12.090
My hope is that someday,

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:15.250
our buildings will actually make us better
than when we walk through hat door.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.635
And maybe someday,

00:09:17.635 --> 00:09:22.904
our offices will remind us all a little
more of our own happy place, thank you.

00:09:22.904 --> 00:09:24.673
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:24.673 --> 00:09:29.050
[MUSIC]

